The government asked former Health Secretary **Alan Milburn** to lead an **independent review** into why nearly **one million** young people are **not in education, employment or training** (often called **NEET**). It has a strong focus on **young people with health conditions and disabilities**.

The **final report**, called **From a Welfare State to a Working State**, was published on **17 November 2026**. Unlike the interim report in May, it makes **recommendations**. Some of the biggest are about **PIP** and **Universal Credit health** for people **under 25**. This guide explains what it proposes, what it does **not** change, and what you can do.

{% callout type="info" title="The short version" %}
- These are **recommendations only**. **Nothing has changed** to PIP, Universal Credit or any other benefit
- For people **under 25**, the report proposes **replacing PIP** and the **Universal Credit health element** with a new system
- It would **abolish the Work Capability Assessment** for this age group
- It promises a legal right to **an opportunity** (a job, course, apprenticeship or placement) and to **support** to take it up
- It says the most **severely disabled** young people should get **more** money and **long-term security**
- It admits some young people could get **less money at first**
- Changes would need **new laws**. The report suggests new claims could start moving to the new system **during this Parliament**
{% /callout %}

## Who the review is about

The review covers people aged **16 to 24**. The final report says:

- nearly **1 in 8** young people are not in education, employment or training
- **disabled** young people are around **3.5 times** more likely to be NEET: **30.2%** compared with **8.6%** of non-disabled young people
- over **400,000** people aged 16 to 24 get **DLA or PIP**, double the number in 2012, and this could reach **820,000** on PIP within ten years
- conditions such as **anxiety, depression, ADHD and autism** now make up almost **2 in 3** new claims from this age group
- more than **7 in 10** young people who are NEET **want to work, learn or train**

The report's main argument is that the system asks young people **what they cannot do** before it asks **what they could do** with the right help, and that too many get stuck on benefits for years. It says **7 in 10** young people who start on health and disability benefits are **still claiming a decade later**.

## The proposed new benefits for under 25s

The report proposes a **separate benefits system for people under 25**. It has five parts.

### 1. Youth Allowance

A new **Youth Allowance** would be the basic income for young people on a low income, **whether or not they have a health condition**.

- it would be set at the **under 25 standard rate of Universal Credit**, with a **higher rate** for young people living independently, such as care leavers
- the **Universal Credit health element** (LCWRA) would be **abolished** for this age group
- the **Work Capability Assessment** would also go, with **no new work test** to replace it
- everyone in work would get a **work allowance**, so they could earn some money before their benefit starts to go down

### 2. A local Health and Disability Fund

A new **local fund**, run by a new **Youth Opportunity Service** in each area, would pay for **extra disability costs** quickly while a young person gets support, for example equipment, travel or daily living costs. It would also help during **serious illness or intensive treatment**, such as cancer treatment, or while longer-term needs are still unclear. Decisions would be made locally, without a long disability assessment.

### 3. Additional Cost Allowance

A new **Additional Cost Allowance** would **replace PIP** for people under 25. It would:

- still be run by the **government**, **not means-tested**, with assessments led by **healthcare professionals**
- be **more closely targeted** at young people whose disability-related extra costs are **significant, established and ongoing**
- be paid **whether you work or not**. Starting a job or a course, or increasing your hours, would **not** reopen the decision
- use **light-touch checks** instead of full reassessments

The report quotes research finding that **21%** of PIP claimants aged 16 to 24 said they had **little in the way of health-related limitations and no extra costs**. It also notes that the separate [Timms Review of PIP](/articles/the-timms-review-of-pip-explained/) concluded PIP is not fit for purpose.

### 4. Severe and Complex Needs Insurance

For a **small number** of young people with the most **profound and lifelong** disabilities, where paid work is not realistic, a new **Severe and Complex Needs Insurance** would pay a **stable, long-term income set above today's maximum benefit rates**. There would be **no qualifying period**, and it should use evidence the state already holds rather than a lengthy assessment. There would be **no expectation to work**, but the Youth Opportunity Service would stay in touch to help with community and local services.

### 5. Child DLA until 18

**Child Disability Living Allowance** would continue until **age 18**, instead of young people moving to PIP at 16. The report says those two years should be used to plan, with the young person and their family, for adult life and work.

{% callout type="warn" title="Some young people could get less at first" %}
The report is open about this. It says the changes "could mean some people get less financial support at the beginning of their benefit claim", and that for many the payment replacing PIP "would pay less cash, at least to begin with". It argues this is outweighed by better long-term chances of work and higher earnings. Disability organisations are likely to challenge this.
{% /callout %}

## A right to an opportunity and to support

Alongside the benefit changes, the report proposes two new **legal guarantees** for every young person aged **18 to 24** who is NEET, whether they claim benefits or not, and from **16** where there is a clear risk.

- the **Opportunity Guarantee**: a route into a job, apprenticeship, college place, work placement, supported internship, volunteering or help to start a business
- the **Support Guarantee**: the health, employment and practical help needed to take that opportunity and keep it

These would be delivered by a new **Youth Opportunity Service** in every area of England by **2029**. The report sets out a timetable:

- a **named adviser** within **14 days**, starting with a conversation about what you want to do, **not** an incapacity assessment
- a personal **Participation Plan** within **4 weeks**
- **health support** underway within **3 months** for those who need it
- something real underway, such as a course or placement, within **4 months**
- a **paid 6-month job** after **12 months** if you are still NEET and work is possible with support. At the moment you have to wait 18 months

## What would be expected of young people

In return, young people getting the Youth Allowance would have a new **duty to engage**: take part where able, make and follow a Participation Plan, and not turn down reasonable offers without good reason.

- these expectations would be **paused** during serious illness, treatment or a crisis
- they would **not apply** to people on Severe and Complex Needs Insurance
- someone who **persistently refuses to engage**, after several attempts to understand why, could **ultimately lose** their Youth Allowance
- the report says sanctions should be **rare** and a **last resort**

## Help for young disabled people to work

The report proposes several changes aimed at disabled young people who want to work:

- **Access to Work** would be brought into the Youth Opportunity Service
- a new **Flexible Jobs Scheme**, based on Denmark, where the state tops up pay for people who can only work a few hours
- more **supported businesses** and **social enterprises** that employ disabled people directly
- employers would get **£3,000** for hiring a young person who has been NEET for 6 months, plus an extra **£2,000** where the young person has substantially greater barriers, such as a disability
- a new government **target to narrow the disability employment gap**, and large employers asked to publish an ambition for employing disabled young people

## Fit notes and mental health

The report also proposes:

- after a **first fit note**, a further fit note for an under 25 would trigger a referral to the Youth Opportunity Service for a work and health conversation. Clinical care would stay with the GP
- any **health-related benefit claim** from someone under 25 would automatically trigger an **offer of support**
- a new **digital mental health service**, including through the NHS App, with **no referral or diagnosis needed**
- **national maximum waiting times** for children and young people's mental health services (CAMHS) by **2028**

## When could this happen?

Nothing would happen straight away. The report suggests:

- the **support and opportunity** should be in place **before** benefits change
- **new claimants** would move to the new system first, with a phased start **during this Parliament**, **subject to new laws**
- **existing claimants** would move when the new service is set up in their area and their **scheduled reassessment** is due, after a **6-month** period of support beforehand
- **current payments would continue** while this happens, so "nobody's income changes before the replacement support is in place"
- the full move should take **no more than 5 years**

The report expects the changes to help around **440,000** young people into lasting work by 2036, and to save **£3.6 billion** a year on benefits by 2036 to 2037. These are the review's own estimates, not government commitments.

## What it does not say

- it is **not** a decision by the government. At the time of writing, the government has not said whether it will accept the benefit proposals
- it does **not** cover people aged **25 and over**. But it asks the government to "act with urgency" on proposals for that group too
- it does **not** say benefits should stop for people who cannot work. It says those who cannot work should get "security", and that the most severely disabled should get **more** help, more quickly
- **Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland** have their own powers over many services, so parts of the plan are written for **England**

{% callout type="good" title="If you get PIP or Universal Credit health now" %}
Nothing has changed because of this report. Keep reporting any changes in your condition as normal. Do not stop claiming, or put off a claim, because of possible future changes. If a decision is wrong, you can still [challenge it](/benefits/pip/challenge-a-decision/).
{% /callout %}

## What the interim report found

The interim report, published in May 2026, set out the problems the final report is trying to fix. It found that:

- only **25%** of young people getting PIP at age 24 are in work
- "At no point in a young person's application or journey on PIP are they asked about, or supported to, work"
- only about **1 in 100** young people in the LCWRA group move into work each month
- young people waiting for a Work Capability Assessment get the equivalent of only **1 to 2 minutes** of work coach time a week
- nearly **half** of health and disability claimants who had not ruled out work were **afraid of losing their benefits** if a job did not work out

Read our guides to [PIP](/benefits/pip/) and [changes to health and disability benefits](/benefits/universal-credit-health/changes/).

## Support that exists now

You do not have to wait for any of these proposals:

- **Access to Work** can pay for support or equipment at work. Read about [Access to Work](/support/work-and-study/access-to-work/)
- **Youth Hubs** and the **Youth Guarantee** offer extra support for young people looking for work
- **Pathways to Work**, **Connect to Work** and **WorkWell** offer work and health support for disabled people and people with health conditions
- under the government's **Right to Try** rules, trying paid or voluntary work will not, in itself, trigger a reassessment

## What you can do now

- **Check you are getting everything you are entitled to.** Try our free [PIP self-check](/benefits/pip/points-checker/)
- **If you want to try work,** ask your work coach or an adviser how it would affect your benefits before you start
- **Get free, independent advice** from Citizens Advice, Turn2us or a local welfare rights service
- **Read the Easy Read summary** of the report on GOV.UK, which is listed in the sources below
- **Have your say.** Disability organisations often run surveys and campaigns when changes are proposed, and you can write to your MP

We will update this page when the government responds.

{% callout type="danger" title="If you are worried or struggling" %}
Uncertainty about benefits can be very stressful. If you are finding it hard to cope, call **Samaritans** free, any time, on **116 123**. If you or someone else is in danger, call **999**.
{% /callout %}
